    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q8a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Suppose $x>0$. Prove that $(1+x)^n>1+nx$ for all $n\\ge2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all integers $n\\ge2$ and $x>0$.\n\nIn the inductive step, $(1+x)^{k+1}>1+(k+1)x+kx^2>1+(k+1)x$.",
          "worked": "Fix $x>0$.\n\nBase case: $(1+x)^2=1+2x+x^2>1+2x$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $(1+x)^k>1+kx$, where $k\\ge2$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}(1+x)^{k+1}&=(1+x)(1+x)^k\\\\(1+x)^{k+1}&>(1+x)(1+kx)\\\\(1+x)^{k+1}&>1+(k+1)x+kx^2\\\\(1+x)^{k+1}&>1+(k+1)x.\\end{align*}$$\nMultiplication preserves the inequality because $1+x>0$, and $kx^2>0$. Therefore, by mathematical induction, the result holds for all integers $n\\ge2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 90
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q8b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that $\\sqrt[n]{n}<2-\\frac1n$ for all $n\\ge2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt[n]{n}<2-\\frac1n$ for all integers $n\\ge2$.\n\nApply part (a) with $x=1-\\frac1n$ and take positive $n$th roots.",
          "worked": "For $n\\ge2$, choose $x=1-\\frac1n>0$. By part (a),\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(2-\\frac1n\\right)^n&=\\left(1+1-\\frac1n\\right)^n\\\\\\left(2-\\frac1n\\right)^n&>1+n\\left(1-\\frac1n\\right)\\\\\\left(2-\\frac1n\\right)^n&>n.\\end{align*}$$\nTaking positive $n$th roots gives $\\sqrt[n]{n}<2-\\frac1n$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      }
    ]
  },
